  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a single-city sample of participants who voluntarily donated their personal Google activity logs.",
      "what": "The authors developed a Feedback Network model using donated Google Search and Location History records from residents in Budapest, Hungary, spanning 2018 to 2022. By adapting the radius of gyration and calculating Concentration Entropy across paired semantic and spatial clusters, the study compared online cognitive exploration with physical movement routines. The analysis revealed that online search behavior is systematically more concentrated around repeated topics than physical mobility is around places, though persistent co-occurring links appeared within retail and professional service activities. Additionally, the COVID-19 pandemic caused a stronger disruption to spatial movement patterns than to online informational routines.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that digital information-seeking operates under narrower behavioral routines than physical urban mobility, establishing a computational method to track how online search and real-world movement reinforce each other over time."
    }
  },
